    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity 5yr CAGR 6.9% diverges >30% from EPS 5yr CAGR 13.3%: book value is depressed by US$0.7-1.5bn/yr of buybacks plus a rising dividend, so equity CAGR understates the compounding. Revenue CAGR (FY21-26 9.6%, incl. ~1-2pp tuck-in M&A) is the growth anchor, as for Visa.",
    "notes": "Owner-FCF series (reported FCF - SBC - equity-award withholding taxes): FY21 1,030.8; FY22 1,066.5; FY23 970.9; FY24 1,357.4; FY25 1,373.2; FY26 1,578.8 (US$M). 5yr owner CAGR 8.9% vs reported 9.1% - withholding taxes grew faster than FCF (74.5 -> 174.3, peak 255.5 in FY25) as a rising share price inflated the tax on option exercises. The 3yr figures start from an FY23 trough (capex and inventory build), so 17.6% overstates the run rate. EPS is diluted and split-adjusted (4:1 split Sep-2024)."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "Cintas keeps compounding as the route-density leader in US uniform rental and facility services: FY2027 lands at the midpoint of raised standalone guidance (+8.4%), then growth fades 0.5pp a year to 4% as new-customer wins from the no-programme segment and first-aid/fire cross-sell mature. Pre-SBC EBITDA margin rises from 29.0% to ~31.8% on SmartTruck routing, the SAP/technology programme and procurement scale, at roughly half the FY22-26 pace of expansion. Cash taxes stay near the guided ~20.5-21% and working capital keeps absorbing ~35c of every incremental revenue dollar (garments in service, receivables, capitalised commissions).",
    "bull": "Cintas sustains high-single-digit organic growth for five-plus years - uniform penetration of the uncovered workforce, first aid and fire protection growing low-teens, pricing holding above cost inflation - and operating leverage from route density plus technology lifts pre-SBC EBITDA margin to ~33.5%, in line with the trajectory of FY22-26. No recession interrupts the path. UniFirst synergies are NOT included even here; they would be additive.",
    "bear": "A US employment downturn hits the core axis - uniform wearers are headcount - as it did in FY2010 (revenue -6%): growth collapses to ~2% in year two, energy, cotton and labour costs squeeze margin back to ~28% and it only recovers to 29%. Separately, UniFirst integration distracts management or the FTC blocks the deal (US$350M reverse fee, not in the flows), and long-run growth settles at 3% as the market saturates. The business stays highly cash generative - there is no solvency risk at <1x net debt/EBITDA."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "Cintas is one of the highest-quality compounders in US services, and the model grants it that: 8% near-term growth, continued margin expansion and a Gordon-bound terminal at 8.5%/3%. The valuation still sits well below the price because the market (~40x trailing earnings, ~50x trailing owner FCF) is capitalising the FY22-26 margin-expansion rate into perpetuity. House basis charges SBC plus the cash withholding taxes on option exercises (~2.7-3.7% of revenue), which the street's 'free cash flow' treats as free.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Employment-cycle exposure: revenue tracks customer headcount; FY2010 revenue fell 6%.",
      "UniFirst merger (~US$5.5bn, ~14.4M new shares + ~US$2.9bn cash) pending FTC review; valued standalone. Synergies would lift value; a block costs a US$350M reverse fee.",
      "Equity-award withholding taxes are volatile (US$74M FY21 -> US$255M FY25) and scale with the share price; the combined SBC charge could run above the 2.5-2.9% path.",
      "Terminal value is ~65-70% of EV; each 1pp of WACC moves value ~20%.",
      "Margin expansion has been the main driver of EPS compounding; a plateau below 30% pre-SBC EBITDA removes most of the base-case margin uplift."
    ]
  },
